<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Phase 11b review round 1, MAJOR 1: app-SCOPED security bookmarks —
	     the reference panel's cross-launch file access (AppSpec §7:
	     `file_bookmark`, local-only under sync). Everything else the app
	     needs (sandbox, outgoing network, user-selected read-write) stays
	     synthesized from the target's ENABLE_* build settings; this file
	     MERGES with them at signing (verified by the codesign dump in the
	     Phase 11b closeout — the EntitlementsTests pin guards the merge). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- 2026-08-30, the 13p signing gap (QUESTIONS #81(a), KnownIssues):
	     the KEYCHAIN IDENTITY. An ACL'd item (Biometric mode's gated store
	     key) or a synchronizable one (the iCloud escrow copy) can only live
	     in the DATA-PROTECTION keychain, and a macOS app is admitted there
	     only with a keychain access group — which is what forces a
	     provisioning profile onto the signature. Without it both writes
	     returned errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018) and every door refused
	     honestly; the same mechanism had been refusing 14b's CF-12 vault
	     biometric gate since 14b.
	     THE VALUE IS THE VARIABLE, DELIBERATELY: Debug builds as
	     io.lduarte.Variorum.debug and Release as io.lduarte.Variorum, and
	     both configurations share THIS file — a literal would break Debug
	     signing and collapse the dev fence. Per-config groups keep the A4
	     dev-store fence whole down to key custody (StoreKey.swift's
	     `Variorum` vs `Variorum-dev` scopes). A group is part of an item's
	     identity and items never migrate between groups, so changing this
	     string later strands what was written under the old one. -->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
		<!-- pre-13x batch 0 (design §3.7, review P8), 2026-09-04: the SHARED
		     group, SECOND — the default group above stays FIRST so every item
		     already written (the gated store key, the bound item, her migrated
		     items) keeps its identity; an item written with no explicit group
		     lands in the FIRST group listed. This one is the same string in
		     Debug, Release and on the iOS target (VariorumMobile.entitlements
		     carries the identical two-entry shape), so an iCloud-synchronizable
		     item written under it on one device is visible to the other — the
		     Standard posture's Apple key (A) is the one item the design writes
		     there, always naming the group explicitly via kSecAttrAccessGroup.
		     The team provisioning profiles permit it by wildcard
		     (keychain-access-groups = TEAMID.*, both platforms — measured at
		     batch 0); a Developer ID build must carry BOTH (KnownIssues, 13r). -->
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)io.lduarte.Variorum.shared</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Phase 13r U2 — SPARKLE'S XPC INSTALLER ROAD (Sparkle's sandboxing
	     doc; the 13r plan's External facts): a sandboxed app reaches the
	     installer through two mach services named after its OWN bundle id —
	     "-spks" (status) and "-spki" (installer connection). THE VALUE IS THE
	     VARIABLE, for the keychain groups' reason above: Debug resolves to
	     io.lduarte.Variorum.debug-spks / -spki and Release to
	     io.lduarte.Variorum-spks / -spki, so the dev fence holds down to the
	     update channel too (and Debug never starts an updater at all —
	     R-13r-7, UpdateChannel.swift). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks</string>
		<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spki</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
